From: Adam Hillson <598804+BadAd84@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] core and glm: TF_GLM_OVN_DISK_SLICE_MIB, a read-ahead disk state restored in slices between rounds

Ours (lane DiskReadSlices, 2026-11-15); nothing ported (the behaviour, not the code, of our Python tier's read slot,
wojo/GLM-5.3-Flash-EXL3-2x-DGX-Sparks-TensorFold dbb90822c). Applies after patches/pr1411 0001-0012.

Why: at TP=3, P=4 / 1M (docs/audit-2026-11-12/disktier-rs2x3da, turn L/t4) a 641,024-token state (4,697 MiB) read
ahead in 3,140 ms on the I/O thread, then stopped the other three decoding streams for 10,851 ms. Where it sits: the
request's log line (ttft 29.507, queued 3.206, prefill 17.563 s; lane_stream.open sets job.began after the lookup) puts
8.74 s between admission and the prompt pass, inside lane_stream.open -> lane_cache_queue.lookup ->
prompt_cache.lookupUserAt -> rows_read.recall -> attempt -> read, whose loop calls cuda_glm_disk.scatter once a file
(2,504 blocks and the state file) on the engine thread; the rest of the gap is the turn's first prompt chunk.
read's own timing excludes it (with a read ahead its line prints the I/O thread's ns).

What: with TF_GLM_OVN_DISK_SLICE_MIB=N (a .glm row, read through platform/environment.zig, default 0 = off), a queued
request whose disk state the read ahead has ready while other requests are admitted stays queued
(lane_host_fetch.held); once a turn of the engine loop (lane_host.run, after rows_host.answer) lane_host_fetch.slice
scatters at most N MiB of it into a blank kept state (rows_slices.step: the read's own plan order, one scatter call a
file's ranges, a range at image byte 0 whole so a family's words open its file), with the store holding the blank's
bytes meanwhile (so a keep beside it sees the room taken). The last slice enters it as a RAM entry the way a lookup's
read back does (rows_read.enter, shared with attempt); the request is then admitted and resumes from RAM. With no
other request admitted the rest goes in one turn; a read the lookup no longer takes, or a restore that cannot begin,
lets the request go to be read at admission as before; a failed slice drops the blank and gives back the hold and the
read (rows_slices.abort); the host's stop drops a restore under way. Off: the same calls in the same order as before
(attempt's choice and entry are moved into rows_read.place and rows_read.enter unchanged; read's counting into
rows_read.counted unchanged).

Bits: the same bytes reach the same image offsets through the same family scatter, only in more calls; GLM's scatter
takes any sub-range (route() splits by rank and own/shared bytes, its seal needs the words whole, which step keeps).
No kernel and no copy routine changes.

Receipts: base tree e567d98959661da15d756103cc2d59051cacb78b (93a2e93ec, patches/engine 0001-0036, patches/pr1411
0001-0012); head tree e8f7f3c74548a445c98da58910aa13a7f1c6ee88, and git apply --check then git apply on a fresh copy of the
base files leaves files equal to that head tree. Host tests on the head tree (zig build test-rows-host, x86_64 WSL, -j2):
-Drows-filter='disk slices' 43/43 passed, 'rows_' 142/142, 'prefetch' 45/45; a filter matches any of its words, so the first
also runs the tree's other names carrying 'disk' or 'slices', and the 13 this patch adds all pass. Lean rule: 0 new
problems (base 6264 files and 386 problems, head 6267 and 386), no file over 600 lines. Mutants (work/DiskReadSlices/
mutants.py, 25 breaks one a behaviour, each applied to a scratch copy whose unmutated run passed the same filter first):
25 of 25 killed, each by a named test. Gates (scripts/gates.sh, GATE_JOBS=4): ds-base bad=0 (test-cuda-host 6319/6349,
30 skipped), ds-head bad=0 (6338/6368, 30 skipped), gate-names ds-base ds-head 0 failing names on either side, lean.sh
0 new. Nothing here ran on a GPU or changed a serve.

